d}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y &=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $0.00$ | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1750bded-03a2-535d-9322-af370f69f5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391221} {:answer/id #uuid "7b95026e-9297-5fe5-b515-53db91622691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b95026e-9297-5fe5-b515-53db91622691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391222} :answer-field/id #uuid "23101e77-153a-53ab-8eaa-fb91f151bfef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4552e55a-71fb-5355-a8ff-c44fe89b0c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224} {:answer/id #uuid "79852ea3-d668-55cf-ae57-0e55042a72c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4552e55a-71fb-5355-a8ff-c44fe89b0c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224} :answer-field/id #uuid "5874e815-f2f7-5127-b199-5dfa1e8dd28e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04b55cbe-b4be-543b-bbe4-7f5a1dc7e192" :question/math-academy-id "q-333614" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afb5eb6-00b1-5824-aac1-9875d7fe66e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305874} {:answer/id #uuid "caf7d461-ff75-53ec-a868-28af19c3a5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307035} {:answer/id #uuid "e46fcc1d-38f3-5984-ae46-bff18bf82d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caf7d461-ff75-53ec-a868-28af19c3a5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307035} :answer-field/id #uuid "09766c90-fc59-51b7-915d-02bb4b00d085" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1aaa80-46dc-5f8f-8c5c-a5903761c3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305248} {:answer/id #uuid "32913ff0-0b12-5e4f-92cb-1a554cc18eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305628} {:answer/id #uuid "34f76203-01ef-5b8f-b51d-78acc82a48e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b1aaa80-46dc-5f8f-8c5c-a5903761c3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305248} :answer-field/id #uuid "304deb61-a235-5743-a7d4-56f1d6f329bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8861ee7-e573-5d09-b7e5-a9dfbae7172d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333620" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97a85038b240896686ef1a796d81acc6aa52d01627f3aeca4268a309badc4867.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bffdd4a-dba1-5509-8e3d-c18282eca373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425300} {:answer/id #uuid "d5288d39-b196-5969-af50-0a4f38dc67d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bffdd4a-dba1-5509-8e3d-c18282eca373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425300} :answer-field/id #uuid "3895b1d1-8fde-5e9c-8254-eb743b48b9d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f3f427d-a11c-5581-802d-503fcc0ff60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425290} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cc45aa-4540-5bbd-9f20-b56a06897950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8cc45aa-4540-5bbd-9f20-b56a06897950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425315} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ba5050-79f5-5038-b180-5f46df5f54fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6510dfcd-a99e-5730-8a08-a25b49094096" :question/math-academy-id "q-333674" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =3$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/95/95bb5be106524711e86d2e1ff9cb21d61141c25eb0a2f8958b51c29791806ccc.png)"}]}
